#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069965 :tutorial/content "So far, we have expressed an exponential equation in logarithm form. We can also go the other way, and express a logarithmic equation in exponential form.\n\nRemember, the base of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\nFor instance, if we know that $\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}},$ we could write this using an exponent as\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c0b02d2-c424-5af8-967f-1b8a92acc6a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
